Advanced data products are only valuable if they are:

Built around real user needs

Intuitive and accessible

Designed to support decision-making

Iteratively improved through feedback

Technical sophistication alone does not create value — usability and relevance do.

2. Why User Requirements Matter in Data Products

Clear user requirements:

Prevent building technically impressive but unused tools

Align product functionality with business goals

Reduce rework and redesign

Improve adoption and stakeholder satisfaction

In the broader module context (AI-enabled, scalable data products), this ensures solutions are not just intelligent — but usable and trusted.

3. Case Study: Enhancing Patient Care

The National Health Service faced challenges predicting patient deterioration.

Identified User Requirements

Real-time clinical data integration

Predictive analytics to flag at-risk patients

Clear, actionable visualisations

Ease of use for clinicians under time pressure

Human-Centric Design Principles Applied

Simplicity in dashboard layout

Accessibility and clarity of metrics

User control and interpretability

Real-time alerts without overload

Outcomes

Reduced ICU mortality

Improved clinician decision-making

Higher user satisfaction

Key Insight: Data products in high-stakes environments must balance advanced analytics with cognitive simplicity.

4. Defining User Requirements in Data Product Development

Effective requirement gathering includes:

User interviews

Stakeholder workshops

Observation of workflows

Persona development

Identifying pain points and success criteria

Strong Requirements Are:

Specific

Measurable

Prioritised

Linked to real user tasks

They should define:

What decisions users need to make

What information they require

What constraints they operate under

What success looks like

5. Human-Centric Design Principles

Human-centric design ensures data products are:

1. Simple and Clear

Avoid clutter

Focus on decision-critical information

Use meaningful visual hierarchy

2. Consistent

Standardised navigation and interactions

Predictable layouts

3. Accessible and Inclusive

Colour contrast compliance

Clear typography

Screen-reader compatibility

Avoiding reliance on colour alone

4. User-Controlled

Filters and drill-down options

Clear feedback mechanisms

Transparent algorithms where possible

6. Implementation: From Requirements to Wireframes

The session activities reinforce practical application:

Activity 1: User Personas & Needs

Translate interviews into personas

Identify core user goals

Map required features to real problems

This ensures the product solves real tasks, not assumed ones.

Activity 2: Wireframing & Iteration

Design dashboard structure based on user needs

Conduct peer usability evaluation

Refine based on feedback

This mirrors real-world agile product development cycles.

7. The Role of Iteration

Human-centric data products are not built once — they evolve.

Key elements:

Prototype early

Gather structured feedback

Test usability

Refine continuously

Iteration reduces risk and improves adoption.

